cff-version: 1.2.0
message: Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its source.
type: software
title: "An uncomputable phase diagram at positive temperature for one classical lattice model"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: '2026-11-10'
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24115745"
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["Gibbs measures", "phase transitions", "undecidability", "uncomputable phase diagram", "lattice models", "Wang tiles", "Ising model", "statistical mechanics"]
abstract: "Can the phase diagram of one fixed classical lattice model be uncomputable? For quantum spin systems at zero temperature it can: there are families of Hamiltonians whose spectral gap or ground-state phase cannot be computed from the parameter. We prove a classical counterpart at positive temperature. There is one translation-invariant nearest-neighbour interaction on ℤ², with local energies 0, 1 and +∞ (hard constraints) and a fixed universal Turing machine U built in, and there are disjoint intervals J_n with rational endpoints computable from n, such that at every inverse temperature β in J_n the model has exactly one translation-invariant Gibbs measure if U does not halt on input n, and at least two if U halts. So uniqueness of the translation-invariant Gibbs measure at rational inverse temperature is undecidable for this single model. Uniqueness among all Gibbs measures fails at every temperature, because the hard constraints freeze a non-periodic background. The background is a hierarchical tiling that contains arbitrarily large square grids. On each grid a Turing machine reads a label n from the size of the grid and runs U on n. Where U halts, small sensors made of free bits and unit energies couple the corner spins of all grids of that size into an ordinary Ising model, whose coupling is large only when β is close to n log 2. Assuming the exact solution of the two-dimensional Ising model, the inverse temperatures with at least two translation-invariant Gibbs measures form exactly a union of explicit intervals around n log 2 over the halting inputs n; restricted to rational β, this set is Σ⁰₁-complete."
